1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,793
VoxPoser is a framework for robotic manipulation that connects natural language with physical action.

2
00:00:05,873 --> 00:00:11,666
Imagine telling a robot, “Close the top drawer.” For a human, this is simple.

3
00:00:11,746 --> 00:00:15,884
For a robot, the instruction must become a physical plan.

4
00:00:15,964 --> 00:00:18,033
Which drawer should it use?

5
00:00:18,113 --> 00:00:20,182
Where should the gripper move?

6
00:00:20,262 --> 00:00:21,917
What should it avoid?

7
00:00:21,997 --> 00:00:25,721
Traditional robotics often solves these questions with task-specific programs.

8
00:00:25,801 --> 00:00:27,870
VoxPoser takes a different approach.

9
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:33,743
It uses language and vision models to construct spatial objectives, then lets a motion

10
00:00:33,823 --> 00:00:36,720
planner turn those objectives into robot motion.

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,938
This video follows that process from language to physical action.

12
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,452
Language is meaningful to humans, but a motion planner needs geometry.

13
00:00:56,532 --> 00:01:02,325
For “Close the top drawer,” the robot needs to locate the drawer, identify where

14
00:01:02,405 --> 00:01:06,129
to interact, determine useful regions, and avoid dangerous ones.

15
00:01:06,209 --> 00:01:09,519
So language must be transformed into spatial information.

16
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:12,082
The language model provides semantic reasoning.

17
00:01:12,162 --> 00:01:16,714
The robotics system then connects that reasoning to the three-dimensional scene.

18
00:01:16,794 --> 00:01:22,587
In short, language describes what should happen, while geometry tells the robot where and

19
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:23,908
how to act.

20
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:31,693
Now let us look at the VoxPoser pipeline.

21
00:01:31,773 --> 00:01:35,889
The system receives a language instruction and an RGB-D observation.

22
00:01:35,969 --> 00:01:41,732
The language model reasons about the instruction and generates code that can query visual

23
00:01:41,812 --> 00:01:43,006
information.

24
00:01:43,086 --> 00:01:46,791
A vision-language model grounds those concepts in the scene.

25
00:01:46,871 --> 00:01:49,341
The result is three-dimensional value maps.

26
00:01:49,421 --> 00:01:53,537
These maps are then given to a model-based motion planner.

27
00:01:53,617 --> 00:01:56,350
The LLM does not directly control the robot.

28
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:00,638
How does language become an actual object in the scene?

29
00:02:00,718 --> 00:02:04,028
Suppose the instruction refers to the top drawer.

30
00:02:04,108 --> 00:02:07,832
The system must identify the relevant object or part.

31
00:02:07,912 --> 00:02:13,706
VoxPoser uses open-vocabulary visual understanding and segmentation to connect the language query with the

32
00:02:13,786 --> 00:02:14,986
observed image.

33
00:02:15,066 --> 00:02:17,548
RGB-D information then provides three-dimensional geometry.

34
00:02:17,628 --> 00:02:23,421
The instruction is now grounded in the physical scene, where the robot can actually

35
00:02:23,501 --> 00:02:24,701
act.

36
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:32,977
A value map can be understood as a function that assigns a value to

37
00:02:33,057 --> 00:02:34,708
locations in three-dimensional space.

38
00:02:34,788 --> 00:02:40,565
For a position x, the value tells the planner how desirable that location is

39
00:02:40,645 --> 00:02:42,295
for a particular purpose.

40
00:02:42,375 --> 00:02:44,851
VoxPoser uses several kinds of maps.

41
00:02:44,931 --> 00:02:46,995
Affordance maps describe useful regions.

42
00:02:47,075 --> 00:02:49,138
Avoidance maps describe undesirable regions.

43
00:02:49,218 --> 00:02:52,932
Other maps can describe rotation, velocity, and gripper actions.

44
00:02:53,012 --> 00:02:57,138
So a value map is more than a colorful visualization.

45
00:02:57,218 --> 00:03:01,450
It is a mathematical representation that connects semantic reasoning with robot planning.

46
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,083
Now we can compose these maps.

47
00:03:04,163 --> 00:03:09,128
Imagine a useful region near a drawer handle and an obstacle nearby.

48
00:03:09,208 --> 00:03:13,346
The affordance map tells the robot where acting is desirable.

49
00:03:13,426 --> 00:03:17,150
The avoidance map tells it where entering is dangerous.

50
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:21,368
The planner combines these spatial objectives into a cost representation.

51
00:03:21,448 --> 00:03:26,828
Conceptually, we want a trajectory that reaches useful regions while avoiding costly ones.

52
00:03:26,908 --> 00:03:30,632
This is the key idea behind composable value maps.

53
00:03:30,712 --> 00:03:36,091
Different spatial functions can be combined and given to a common motion planner.

54
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:56,479
Once the value maps are available, the robot still needs an actual trajectory.

55
00:03:56,559 --> 00:03:58,214
Imagine several candidate paths.

56
00:03:58,294 --> 00:04:00,363
One passes through an obstacle.

57
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:02,926
Another is safe but unnecessarily expensive.

58
00:04:03,006 --> 00:04:07,972
A third path safely reaches the target while satisfying the spatial objective.

59
00:04:08,052 --> 00:04:13,431
VoxPoser uses model-based planning to search for a sequence of collision-free end-effector positions.

60
00:04:13,511 --> 00:04:15,994
The value maps provide the objective.

61
00:04:16,074 --> 00:04:18,557
The motion planner provides the trajectory.

62
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:36,024
A manipulation trajectory is more than just XYZ position.

63
00:04:36,104 --> 00:04:38,173
The end-effector also has orientation.

64
00:04:38,253 --> 00:04:44,046
Depending on the task, we may care about velocity and whether the gripper is

65
00:04:44,126 --> 00:04:45,368
open or closed.

66
00:04:45,448 --> 00:04:48,758
VoxPoser represents these properties with different value maps.

67
00:04:48,838 --> 00:04:51,734
Position tells the robot where to move.

68
00:04:51,814 --> 00:04:55,125
Orientation tells it how the tool should face.

69
00:04:55,205 --> 00:04:56,860
Velocity describes motion characteristics.

70
00:04:56,940 --> 00:05:01,078
Gripper actions determine when the fingers should open or close.

71
00:05:01,158 --> 00:05:06,123
Together, these components turn a spatial plan into an executable manipulation action.

72
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:09,783
Physical environments are not perfectly predictable.

73
00:05:09,863 --> 00:05:14,001
Objects may move, and contact may behave differently than expected.

74
00:05:14,081 --> 00:05:16,150
VoxPoser therefore uses closed-loop execution.

75
00:05:16,230 --> 00:05:22,023
The system observes the environment, plans, executes part of the action, and observes again.

76
00:05:22,103 --> 00:05:25,413
New visual information can then update the plan.

77
00:05:25,493 --> 00:05:28,390
The paper reports replanning at five hertz.

78
00:05:28,470 --> 00:05:33,849
This allows the robot to adapt instead of blindly following its first plan.

79
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,524
VoxPoser is evaluated in both simulation and real-robot environments.

80
00:05:48,604 --> 00:05:54,397
The experiments test whether natural-language instructions can produce useful behavior across different tasks and

81
00:05:54,477 --> 00:05:55,677
objects.

82
00:05:55,757 --> 00:06:01,550
The framework supports free-form instructions and open-set objects rather than requiring a separate program

83
00:06:01,630 --> 00:06:02,872
for every command.

84
00:06:02,952 --> 00:06:08,745
This flexibility is important for Physical AI, where robots encounter situations that cannot all

85
00:06:08,825 --> 00:06:09,950
be manually scripted.

86
00:06:10,100 --> 00:06:12,997
One interesting capability is behavioral commonsense reasoning.

87
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:14,732
Consider a table-setting task.

88
00:06:14,812 --> 00:06:20,605
If the user says, “I am left-handed,” that statement provides context that can influence

89
00:06:20,685 --> 00:06:23,168
how the table should be arranged.

90
00:06:23,248 --> 00:06:28,627
The language model can use that context to infer a different spatial preference.

91
00:06:28,707 --> 00:06:34,086
That decision can then be expressed through the same value-map and planning framework.

92
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,010
VoxPoser can also respond to fine-grained language corrections.

93
00:06:40,090 --> 00:06:45,883
Imagine positioning a teapot lid and receiving the instruction, “You are off by one

94
00:06:45,963 --> 00:06:50,515
centimeter.” That small linguistic correction corresponds to a small physical correction.

95
00:06:50,595 --> 00:06:56,388
The system can reinterpret the instruction and update the spatial objective instead of requiring

96
00:06:56,468 --> 00:06:58,123
a new task-specific policy.

97
00:06:58,203 --> 00:07:02,755
This makes language a flexible interface even for precise physical adjustments.

98
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:09,283
Another capability is multi-step visual reasoning.

99
00:07:09,363 --> 00:07:13,501
Consider the instruction to open a drawer precisely by half.

100
00:07:13,581 --> 00:07:17,719
The robot may not know the exact displacement model beforehand.

101
00:07:17,799 --> 00:07:23,592
It can open the drawer, observe how far the handle moved, use that measurement,

102
00:07:23,672 --> 00:07:27,396
and then act again to reach the desired midpoint.

103
00:07:27,476 --> 00:07:32,441
The task becomes a sequence of observation, measurement, action, and new observation.

104
00:07:32,521 --> 00:07:38,314
The system can therefore use interaction to obtain information that was not known at

105
00:07:38,394 --> 00:07:39,594
the beginning.

106
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:53,524
Some physical interactions are difficult to model in advance.

107
00:07:53,604 --> 00:07:58,983
Contact, friction, and object motion can behave differently from a simple geometric prediction.

108
00:07:59,063 --> 00:08:02,788
VoxPoser also explores learning dynamics online from visual observations.

109
00:08:02,868 --> 00:08:08,661
The robot can observe how an object reacts during interaction and use that information

110
00:08:08,741 --> 00:08:10,396
to improve later planning.

111
00:08:10,476 --> 00:08:15,441
The robot does not necessarily need a perfect dynamics model before acting.

112
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:23,516
So what is the main contribution of VoxPoser?

113
00:08:23,596 --> 00:08:27,491
It is not simply an LLM connected to a robot.

114
00:08:27,571 --> 00:08:33,025
The key bridge combines language and vision reasoning, three-dimensional value maps, and model-based planning.

115
00:08:33,105 --> 00:08:35,052
First, the system interprets language.

116
00:08:35,132 --> 00:08:38,248
Second, it converts that interpretation into spatial objectives.

117
00:08:38,328 --> 00:08:41,444
Third, it uses those objectives to synthesize motion.

118
00:08:41,524 --> 00:08:44,550
These value maps connect semantic reasoning with physical planning.

119
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:48,424
The whole framework can be summarized as a chain.

120
00:08:48,504 --> 00:08:50,573
Language describes the desired behavior.

121
00:08:50,653 --> 00:08:55,205
Language and vision models interpret that behavior in the current scene.

122
00:08:55,285 --> 00:08:59,837
Value maps describe useful and undesirable regions and other action properties.

123
00:08:59,917 --> 00:09:03,641
A motion planner converts those objectives into a trajectory.

124
00:09:03,721 --> 00:09:07,859
The robot then executes while continuing to observe its environment.

125
00:09:07,939 --> 00:09:12,077
This is how high-level reasoning interacts with physical robot control.

126
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:14,765
The experiments also provide quantitative evidence.

127
00:09:14,845 --> 00:09:20,597
In the real-world evaluation, VoxPoser achieved about eighty-eight percent success in static settings and

128
00:09:20,677 --> 00:09:22,731
about seventy percent under disturbances.

129
00:09:22,811 --> 00:09:27,330
The action-primitives baseline performed much worse, including zero percent under disturbances.

130
00:09:27,410 --> 00:09:31,518
This matters because physical robots must deal with changing environments.

131
00:09:31,598 --> 00:09:36,950
Closed-loop language-driven planning is therefore not only flexible, but also more robust during execution.

132
00:09:37,100 --> 00:09:42,066
The authors evaluate VoxPoser in simulation as well as on real robots.

133
00:09:42,146 --> 00:09:47,939
Simulation allows many tasks and conditions to be tested systematically, including seen and unseen

134
00:09:48,019 --> 00:09:49,219
settings.

135
00:09:49,299 --> 00:09:51,781
Real-robot experiments provide a different test.

136
00:09:51,861 --> 00:09:56,827
They expose the system to real sensors, physical contact, and execution uncertainty.

137
00:09:56,907 --> 00:10:00,631
Together, these experiments test both generalization and physical execution.

138
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,997
VoxPoser does not eliminate every failure mode.

139
00:10:04,077 --> 00:10:07,801
The pipeline still depends on several components working correctly.

140
00:10:07,881 --> 00:10:10,777
A perception error can produce incorrect grounding.

141
00:10:10,857 --> 00:10:14,168
Incorrect grounding can produce a bad value map.

142
00:10:14,248 --> 00:10:17,972
A bad value map can produce an undesirable trajectory.

143
00:10:18,052 --> 00:10:21,776
Language ambiguity and unpredictable contact can also cause failures.

144
00:10:21,856 --> 00:10:27,235
So VoxPoser reduces task-specific engineering, but it does not make robotics completely automatic.

145
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:31,124
Let us run through the complete pipeline once more.

146
00:10:31,204 --> 00:10:33,687
A user provides a language instruction.

147
00:10:33,767 --> 00:10:37,905
The language model interprets it and generates a spatial program.

148
00:10:37,985 --> 00:10:42,123
Visual understanding grounds the relevant concepts in the current scene.

149
00:10:42,203 --> 00:10:44,686
The system constructs composable value maps.

150
00:10:44,766 --> 00:10:48,903
Those maps encode affordance, avoidance, orientation, velocity, and gripper information.

151
00:10:48,983 --> 00:10:52,294
The motion planner converts them into a trajectory.

152
00:10:52,374 --> 00:10:56,926
The robot executes the action and continues to observe the environment.

153
00:10:57,006 --> 00:11:00,316
This cycle connects language reasoning with physical behavior.

154
00:11:10,600 --> 00:11:13,497
Why is this interesting for Physical AI?

155
00:11:13,577 --> 00:11:18,542
The system connects reasoning about the world with acting in the world.

156
00:11:18,622 --> 00:11:21,932
Language provides a flexible way to express goals.

157
00:11:22,012 --> 00:11:25,737
Three-dimensional value maps connect those goals to spatial structure.

158
00:11:25,817 --> 00:11:29,127
Motion planning converts that structure into physical behavior.

159
00:11:29,207 --> 00:11:35,000
The key insight is that language reasoning can become a mathematical objective that a

160
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:36,735
robotics system can optimize.

161
00:11:42,900 --> 00:11:48,693
The language model does not need to invent a complete robot controller from scratch.

162
00:11:48,773 --> 00:11:51,670
Instead, it can work with functional primitives.

163
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:57,543
For example, the program can parse the query object, request affordance maps, request avoidance

164
00:11:57,623 --> 00:12:01,347
maps, compose the maps, and then plan a trajectory.

165
00:12:01,427 --> 00:12:05,151
The language model acts as a high-level program synthesizer.

166
00:12:05,231 --> 00:12:08,128
The robotics framework provides the actual operations.

167
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:19,083
Zero-shot does not mean zero computation.

168
00:12:19,163 --> 00:12:23,714
The system still performs visual grounding, value-map construction, optimization, and control.

169
00:12:23,794 --> 00:12:29,588
Here, zero-shot means that new instructions or objects can be handled without task-specific additional

170
00:12:29,668 --> 00:12:30,868
training.

171
00:12:30,948 --> 00:12:34,672
A new command can produce a new spatial program.

172
00:12:34,752 --> 00:12:38,062
The planner can then synthesize a new trajectory.

173
00:12:38,142 --> 00:12:43,935
The system generates solutions at inference time instead of relying on a separately trained

174
00:12:44,015 --> 00:12:45,670
policy for every task.

175
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,910
We can now summarize VoxPoser in one idea.

176
00:12:54,990 --> 00:12:57,473
Language tells us what should happen.

177
00:12:57,553 --> 00:13:00,863
Spatial reasoning determines where the relevant regions are.

178
00:13:00,943 --> 00:13:04,668
Composable value maps represent those objectives in three-dimensional space.

179
00:13:04,748 --> 00:13:08,058
Motion planning turns them into a physical trajectory.

180
00:13:08,138 --> 00:13:12,276
Closed-loop observation lets the robot adapt when the world changes.

181
00:13:12,356 --> 00:13:17,735
That is the bridge VoxPoser builds between language reasoning and physical robot action.

